      "question": "Transformer 1 at MAIN has some dbds in its oil, and I need to know if corrosive sulfur could be causing discharges in the paper. Look at dbds over the available window alongside the latest hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene. Classify the fault from those five gases, and check co and co2 for paper involvement. Then tell me whether the picture fits copper sulfide deposition driving 'Partial Discharge in Oil' or 'Low-Energy Arcing (Sparking)', or whether it looks more like plain 'Oil Dielectric Degradation', using dielectric_rigidity and interfacial_v as supporting evidence. Give me a verdict with a confidence level and the two strongest pieces of evidence behind it, and recommend whether we should consider a passivator treatment or an oil change. Base the sulfur judgement on dbds only, not on the gases. If dbds has no data in the window, say that corrosive sulfur can't be assessed, and give the discharge versus degradation verdict from the gases and oil quality alone.",
      "answer": "**Verdict: this doesn't look like copper sulfide driving discharges, and it doesn't look like plain oil dielectric degradation either. The gases point to a severe high-temperature thermal fault (above 700 °C) in the oil, with little paper involvement.**\n\n**Confidence:** High that there is no partial discharge or sparking. Moderate-to-high that the fault is thermal and above 700 °C, because one of the Rogers ratios sits right on its boundary (see below). The DGA interpretation tool was unavailable, so I worked out the Rogers ratios and Duval percentages by hand.\n\n**Data window:** 5 daily samples, 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19.\n\n**Corrosive sulfur (judged on dbds only):** dbds was steady at 18–20 ppm (mean 19). That is above the roughly 5–10 ppm level often treated as a concern for corrosive sulfur, so copper sulfide deposition is a real latent risk for this oil.\n\n**Fault classification (latest sample: H2 2850, CH4 7400, C2H6 5470, C2H4 16650, C2H2 7 ppm):**\n\n| Rogers ratio | Value | Code |\n|---|---|---|\n| C2H2/C2H4 | 0.0004 | 0 |\n| CH4/H2 | 2.60 | 2 |\n| C2H4/C2H6 | 3.04 | 2 (just over the boundary of 3) |\n\n- The code 0‑2‑2 means a thermal fault above 700 °C.\n- The Duval triangle agrees: 31% CH4, 69% C2H4 and 0.03% C2H2 falls in zone T3.\n\n**Paper involvement:** CO is only 30–35 ppm and CO2 is 1300–1350 ppm, so CO2/CO is about 42. A ratio that far above 10 doesn't point to paper in the fault; it's more consistent with normal oxidation.\n\n**Oil quality:**\n- Dielectric strength (dielectric_rigidity) is 53–56 kV, which is good.\n- Interfacial tension (interfacial_v) is 44–46, which is good.\n- Water content is 0–1 ppm.\n\nNone of these shows dielectric degradation.\n\n**Two strongest pieces of evidence:**\n1. **The gas pattern rules out discharges.** Partial discharge would show hydrogen dominant with CH4/H2 below 0.1; here it is 2.6. Sparking would show acetylene high relative to ethylene; here C2H2 is only 7 ppm and C2H2/C2H4 is 0.0004. Ethylene dominates, which is the signature of a hot spot.\n2. **The oil's insulating quality is intact.** Stable dielectric strength of 53–56 kV, interfacial tension of 44–46 and near-zero water all argue against oil dielectric degradation.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- **Deal with the thermal fault first.** Ethylene at about 16,650 ppm and methane at about 7,400 ppm are very high. The oil temperature alarm and trip flags are also set, with oil temperature (OTI) at 84–87 °C. Look for a hot spot such as a bad joint or connection, tap-changer contacts, or circulating currents in the core. Increase DGA sampling frequency in the meantime.\n- **Passivator treatment is reasonable as a short-term measure** because dbds is around 19 ppm. The high temperatures speed up copper sulfide formation, so there is a case for acting now. Monitor the passivator level afterwards, because a hot spot uses it up faster and passivators can themselves cause some stray gassing.\n- **An oil change isn't justified on oil quality.** Plan permanent dbds removal (an oil change or selective dbds removal) for the outage when the thermal fault is repaired.",
